{
  "title": "Frontal hip excursion during stance in two symptomatic cohorts",
  "measure": "Adduction/abduction range over 20\u201380% of normalized stance (degrees)",
  "sources": {
    "gtpsDataset": "https://doi.org/10.17632/66mcwyk854.1",
    "gtpsPaper": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gaitpost.2019.02.031",
    "gtpsWorkbookSha256": "95f2b5fa6ac78367eddc4ce294c235e62f49a21169df70a9f8e2bed35a5470d6",
    "oaDataset": "https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.13656233.v4",
    "oaMetadata": "https://doi.org/10.6084/m9.figshare.13655975.v10",
    "oaPaper": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-022-01483-3",
    "license": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"
  },
  "method": [
    "Untaped baseline GTPS workbook, RHipAddAbd, one 101-point affected-foot stance trace per numeric ID.",
    "Female preoperative OA patients, affected side only. Deduplicate repeated angle curves within trials. Require a complete interior force contact on the same time grid. Interpolate each loaded stance to 101 points and average across contacts within a participant before measuring excursion.",
    "Use max minus min within 20\u201380% of normalized stance. This omits contact edges and is invariant to a constant angle offset or left/right sign reversal. Repeat over 10\u201390% and 0\u2013100%.",
    "10,000 independent within-cohort participant bootstrap resamples. Shared central range is the intersection of the two empirical 10th\u201390th percentile intervals."
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Diagnosis and laboratory coincide. The GTPS site used the UWA model and the OA site used Plug-in Gait. Differences in coordinate systems, force gating, recruitment and severity remain.",
    "The GTPS workbook supplies 51 unique baseline IDs and curves. The publication reports 50 women. All complete records are retained and single-record omission sensitivity is shown.",
    "Both cohorts walked barefoot at their preferred speed. Individual GTPS ages and walking speeds are unavailable in the gait workbook, precluding individual age and speed matching.",
    "The observations describe selected women and group overlap. Coexisting GTPS in OA is unknown and cross-site differences cannot establish clinical classification performance."
  ],
